Whitney Hodges: Mm-hmm.

00:06:19.000 --> 00:06:23.000
Catherine Sugden: I think, like, the LES, she was kind of curious about…

00:06:23.000 --> 00:06:28.000
Catherine Sugden: You know what you guys offer, what you suggest, it's mostly like.

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:27.000
Whitney Hodges: Yeah.

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:41.000
Catherine Sugden: Inquiry-wise, our inquiries are a little low. We are working with Sarah from SE… Sarah does SEO. She's… she's been great, so we just started setting up with her, so that should be helpful, too, so I think we're just kind of really…

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:31.000
Whitney Hodges: No.

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:36.000
Whitney Hodges: Great, good. Mm-hmm.

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:47.000
Catherine Sugden: Trying to, like, figure out, like, our algorithm and how to get more…

00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:47.000
Whitney Hodges: Great.

00:06:47.000 --> 00:06:49.000
Catherine Sugden: Um…

00:06:47.000 --> 00:06:49.000
Whitney Hodges: Mm-hmm.

00:06:49.000 --> 00:06:55.000
Catherine Sugden: Qualified leads, so, um, yeah, just kind of being more of a presence, so…

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:52.000
Whitney Hodges: Mm-hmm.

00:06:55.000 --> 00:07:14.000
Whitney Hodges: Okay, love it. Well, we definitely specialize on the paid advertising side of things, specifically with Google and Meta. I know you mentioned TikTok, and TikTok is amazing, but we do really recommend focusing efforts there more on the.

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:02.000
Catherine Sugden: Yes.

00:07:07.000 --> 00:07:09.000
Catherine Sugden: Yes.

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:28.000
Whitney Hodges: Organic side. So kind of sounds like similar to what what Brittany's doing in the in the planning business. So that may be a route that you want to go for the Illia. But I would recommend, then pairing that right with.

00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:17.000
Catherine Sugden: Okay.

00:07:23.000 --> 00:07:24.000
Catherine Sugden: Okay.

00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:48.000
Whitney Hodges: the organic Google, like you're already doing with Sarah, and then paid on Google, and then paid on Meta as well. Because what we really find is that when you're doing paid advertising on both Google and Meta, that it really, like, amplifies things and creates a system where it's all kind of working together. So.

00:07:31.000 --> 00:07:33.000
Catherine Sugden: Then…

00:07:35.000 --> 00:07:36.000
Catherine Sugden: Okay.

00:07:48.000 --> 00:08:04.000
Whitney Hodges: Like, for example, you know, if you had $1,000 a month to spend on your paid advertising, you're typically going to see that go farther when you're splitting it between Google and Meta than if you were to just say, okay, I want to spend $1,000 a month on Google ads, or I want to spend $1,000 a month on Meta ads.

00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:22.000
Whitney Hodges: ads, right? Because the way that people use those two platforms is different, right? Like, people go to Google to search, like, they're going there with high intent. They're like, okay, I want to find a wedding venue in this area or a wedding venue near me, right?

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:13.000
Catherine Sugden: Okay.

00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:37.000
Whitney Hodges: Versus on, you know, Instagram or Facebook, a lot of times they're just kind of getting on there to scroll, right? To connect with people, to be entertained. And so it's more disruptor marketing on that side, where it's like.

00:08:29.000 --> 00:08:31.000
Catherine Sugden: Yes. Yeah.

00:08:37.000 --> 00:08:52.000
Whitney Hodges: an ad crosses their newsfeed or their stories and really grabs their attention, and maybe it's, you know, a venue or a vendor that they've never heard of, but they're like, oh my god, this looks amazing, I want to check it out, right? They can also kind of continue to work together as well.

00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:50.000
Catherine Sugden: Mm-hmm.

00:08:52.000 --> 00:09:13.000
Whitney Hodges: Because we know that when people are searching on Google, whether they find you organically or whether they find you through an ad, oftentimes in the beginning, they may still kind of be in that research phase, right? So they're checking out the website or checking out the landing page. They're comparing options. They may not be ready to inquire right at that moment.

00:09:12.000 --> 00:09:14.000
Catherine Sugden: Yeah.

00:09:13.000 --> 00:09:35.000
Whitney Hodges: But when we're layering meta ads in with it, we can set it up to where there are retargeting ads running so that the algorithm, the paid algorithm, basically knows, oh, okay, this user visited the ILIA's website recently. When they open up their Instagram, let me show them an ad for the ILIA to remind them, right? So it kind of creates this almost like reminder system.

00:09:27.000 --> 00:09:28.000
Catherine Sugden: Mm-hmm.

00:09:35.000 --> 00:09:55.000
Whitney Hodges: and really helps build that like no like trust factor to get more inquiries coming in. But also, even after somebody's inquired right for them to kind of continue seeing your ads as they're making that decision right and and deciding, you know, kind of between different places where they're touring and and making final decisions.

00:09:46.000 --> 00:09:47.000
Catherine Sugden: Yes.

00:09:55.000 --> 00:09:56.000
Catherine Sugden: Mm-hmm.

00:09:55.000 --> 00:09:57.000
Whitney Hodges: Things like that.

00:09:57.000 --> 00:09:58.000
Whitney Hodges: Yeah.

00:09:57.000 --> 00:09:59.000
Catherine Sugden: Yeah. Okay. Awesome.

00:09:59.000 --> 00:10:00.000
Whitney Hodges: Mm-hmm.

00:09:59.000 --> 00:10:03.000
Catherine Sugden: Um, what type… so…

00:10:03.000 --> 00:10:07.000
Catherine Sugden: With, like, Google Meta ads, like, what…

00:10:07.000 --> 00:10:16.000
Catherine Sugden: Kind of like, what's your, like, your strategy, I guess? And, like, what… what do you see do… like, do best for…

00:10:11.000 --> 00:10:12.000
Whitney Hodges: Hmm. Okay.

00:10:15.000 --> 00:10:17.000
Whitney Hodges: Mm-hmm.

00:10:16.000 --> 00:10:21.000
Catherine Sugden: inquiries purposes, I guess, is kind of the question.

00:10:20.000 --> 00:10:43.000
Whitney Hodges: Yeah, no, good question. So the strategy essentially is to send all of your ads traffic to a dedicated landing page. So as opposed to sending them to like the full-blown website where they can explore and check things out, which is great. I mean, that is great for organic traffic, right? Especially when people are in research mode. But when you're actually paying for.

00:10:43.000 --> 00:10:59.000
Whitney Hodges: those people to see your ads, right, and paying for that traffic, we want to make sure that we can make the most of it, right, and can, like, have the highest likelihood of converting those people into actual inquiries. So that's why we recommend sending them to a long-form.

00:10:59.000 --> 00:11:15.000
Whitney Hodges: landing page. So, essentially, it takes all of the great elements of your website, but puts it on one long, streamlined page with consistent and repeated calls to action to get people to inquire, right? So, as opposed to, like, a website where.

00:11:15.000 --> 00:11:31.000
Whitney Hodges: Oftentimes, it's like, check out our galleries, or go to our blog, or view our pricing, like, all of these different sort of things, paths that they can go, it's just one next step. The one next step being, like, inquire with us so you can get your tour booked. Okay, here's Brittany, let me let her in.

00:11:22.000 --> 00:11:24.000
Catherine Sugden: Mm-hmm.

